{
  "id": "events-conference-flagship",
  "name": "TechFwd Summit — conference production company (flagship + city roadshows)",
  "family": "events",
  "inspiration": "notion.com/templates/category/event-planning — the 'Event Planning OS' / conference-producer stack: program grid, speaker roster, sponsor tiers, exhibitor booths, volunteer shift board, vendor + budget line ledger. Naming decoration (emoji, city/year suffixes, a trailing space) copied from the real 37-database workspace ('Sponsors 💰' in the manner of 'Recipe SB' / 'Meal Calendar DB').",
  "notes": "A B2B conference producer with a real ops team: one flagship annual summit (multi-track program, sponsors, exhibitors, volunteers, vendors, budget) plus a cut-down version of the same session grid taken on the road as single-day city stops. The roadshow session database was duplicated per city and lightly trimmed, which is the professional-scale version of the duplicate-template problem. Exercises decision points 1 (exact-ish duplicate databases), 2 (same concept, disjoint schemas), 4 (same property name with identical options across unrelated types), 6 (bundled whitelist hits), 9 (scale: 2 properties vs 13) and 10 (messy real-world naming).\n\nOPTION DRIFT INSIDE A MERGED KIND. The three session databases carry the SAME 'Room' select under three locally re-optioned vocabularies (Hall A/Hall B/Studio 1/Studio 2/Atrium vs Kesselhaus/Salon/Rooftop vs Main Stage/The Loft/Mezzanine), and the roadshows disagree on 'City' too. That is exactly what happens when a duplicated template gets re-optioned per venue. Nothing is asserted about Room or City on purpose: kind-merging MUST survive option divergence (the three are one kind regardless), while relation-merging need not — whether the three Room selects collapse to one relation with a fifteen-value vocabulary or stay three is a defensible judgement call either way, so it stays out of expect. 'Track 🎤', 'Session Type' and 'Tags' are kept byte-identical across all three so the merge signal is unambiguous.\n\nDELIBERATELY UNASSERTED. (a) The 'Slides Received', 'Travel Booked', 'Contract Signed' and 'Paid' checkboxes are arguable completion flags; mapping any of them to bundled `done` is taste, not correctness, so none is asserted in either direction. (b) 'Status' appears with three unrelated vocabularies (session lifecycle, speaker invitation funnel, sponsorship pipeline); status-vocabulary splitting is another fixture's job. (c) db-program 'Tags' (skill level) and db-marketing 'Tags' (campaign phase) share a name and format but not a vocabulary — only the marketing one is asserted onto bundled `tag`; whether the session one follows is left open. (d) 'Capacity' (number) appears in both db-program and db-venues, and 'Contact Phone' in both db-sponsors and db-venues — plausible shares, but not worth baking in.",
